A||N||12||maninblack||3.1LV6 conversion on the 2.8L block||||5||
Z||000000||maninblack||08-27-2000||05:03 AM||||can anyone give me some idea where I can get<BR>info for having my 87SEV6 5sp 2.8L convert to<BR>3.1L or higher using the same block?thx||||reg||5
Z||000001||ur2biteme||08-27-2000||06:27 AM||||PLEASE GUYS TELL ME IF I'M WRONG!  if i remember right, the only difference between the 2.8 and the 3.1 is the crank, giving it a longer stroke with the same bore.  now larger?  not so sure there.  but i think that the 3.4 (camaro) is the 3.1 with a larger head, but a little more work to squeeze in because of needing to redrill the starter location.  if i'm right, i hope it helps.  <P>i'm wanting to do a similar swap in about a year, but i've got a lot more work cut out for me, being that i've got an '87 with the duke (wiring surgery! joy!).<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>165k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||08-27-2000||07:14 AM||||Crank, pistons and on pre 88s the flywheel.<BR>talk to your local engine rebuilder||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||08-27-2000||11:45 AM||||Do a search on this forum; there is a TON of information here and this subject has been talked to death.<P>The difference is the stroked crankshaft from a 3.1 motor and different dished pistons. The cast iron heads are the same. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||08-27-2000||01:53 PM||||get a distributer 3.1 crank (not the magnetic ignition one) get the pistons from a 3.1 iron head engine (not the aluminum heads, iron head pistons have a like .010 dish, alum have .020 or something.) that'll put you at like 8.85:1 compression, and exactly a 3.1. If ya bore it over, you can bump it up to a 3.2 (someone else can do the math, but it's like .04 or .05 over) just be careful, there was someone out here who went to .060 over and then couldn't find pistons. I beleive it is the '88 flywheel you need, I lucked out and have an '88 so I didn't worry about that one as much. The cam on this set up will make all the difference tho, the stock fiero 2.8 cam will get you TONS of torque (over 200 fl/plbs) but not much of a boost in hp (arround 145?), My machinest found one that I think should be good, look for the thread "cam for 3.2" err 3.1 err something. <P>as far as differences between the 2.8 and 3.1, it's just crank and pistons. the bore is the same, but the 3.1 has a longer stroke. to make up for the extra swing w/ the same connecting rods, the pistons have their wrist pins located higher.<P>hope that helps, if you have any questions, just ask, I've been battling this one out for the last few months, I get the engine back in just over 2 weeks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||maninblack||08-27-2000||03:42 PM||||I just want to thx everyone for their info.<BR>I am new to the fiero family but so far,I am<BR>having a great time with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||ggcom||08-27-2000||09:10 PM||||The 3.4L pushrod engine, used in the <BR>'93-95 f-bodies is just slightly different than the 2.8 in a couple ways:<BR>-same basic block, uses 3.1 crank, but is bored larger.<BR>-has re-enforced exterior block walls, versus the smooth sides on the 2.8/3.1<BR>-oil filter side, lowest bellhousing bolt<BR>hole area is a sharp angel, vs. the 2.8/3.1 concurved shape.  This presents a problem when mated with fiero auto tranny.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||08-28-2000||08:49 AM||||I believe some of the very early Fiero v-6s had smaller main bearings.||||reg||1
